id: pm-xm0id4
title: "Closing an item deletes its ordering edges, so the historical ordering graph is structurally unable to exist"
description: "clearClosedBlockerSignals in src/cli/commands/close.ts:600-644 removes every dependency row whose kind is blocked_by when an item is closed: existingDeps.filter((dep) => dep.kind !== \"blocked_by\") at line 626. The filter is unconditional. It does not check whether the blocker is still open, it does not check how many blocked_by rows exist, and it runs on the close of the DEPENDENT item, which is the exact moment the ordering fact becomes historical rather than actionable.\n\nVerified in an isolated sandbox (PM_PATH and PM_GLOBAL_PATH overridden, 2026-07-31). Item D was given three blocked_by rows to A, B and C plus one related row to A, and all three blockers were left OPEN. pm deps D reported edge_count 1 and pm graph analyze reported layer_count 2 with frontier_count 1, so the ordering was recorded and the analyzer agreed. Closing D with an unchanged blocker set left exactly one dependency row: the related edge. All three ordering edges to still-unsatisfied prerequisites were deleted. A second sandbox run isolated the trigger to the dependent's own close rather than the blocker's: closing the blocker A left the edge intact and pm deps still reported edge_count 1; closing the dependent B dropped edge_count to 0 and moved component_count from 1 to 2.\n\nThe immutable record proves both the edge and its deletion. The close history entry lists dependencies among its changed_fields, and pm get <id> --at 2 reconstructs the blocked_by row with reconstructed true. The information is therefore recoverable from history and absent from every projection that graph analytics, readiness and scheduling read, which are all built on current item state.\n\nCorpus measurement over every tracked item file, 2026-07-31, gives the population-level signature of the deletion. Of 1,900 closed items exactly ONE retains a blocked_by row; 91 of 318 open items hold one. The reverse spelling of the same relation, blocks, is not touched by this code path because blocker resolution only matches the blocked_by literal, and it shows the mirror-image distribution: 189 closed items hold a blocks row against 4 open ones. The same real prerequisite therefore survives closure roughly 200 times more often when it happens to be written from the blocker endpoint.\n\nThis reframes pm-gxs55a. That item measures frontier_count 1845 of 2104 ordered nodes and attributes the flat ordering graph to a corpus that records what work belongs to what and almost never what had to happen before what. The measurement is right and the attribution is not: ordering WAS authored, at least a few hundred times over the project's life, and the close path deleted it every time on the endpoint the scheduler can see. This is a deletion defect, not an authoring-discipline gap.\n\nIt also makes pm-ye46ns unshippable as specified. That tranche recovers sequence edges for TERMINAL work from explicit prerequisite statements and writes them as blocked_by. Every such write lands on a closed item, so it is one lifecycle transition away from being deleted again, and re-closing after any reopen erases the recovered tranche silently.\n\nThe product is left with no way to record an ordering constraint that is both actionable while open and durable after close. blocked_by is visible to readiness and destroyed at close. blocks survives close and is invisible to readiness, which is what pm-jkbqt8 proves. Whichever endpoint an author picks, exactly one of the two properties is lost."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[5]: data-loss,graph,history,lifecycle,ordering
