comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T14:40:28.597Z","harness:codex","Bundled with pm-z5pmf8 and pm-2ew0w3: preserve historical predecessor context while making the close policy shared by CLI, SDK, and MCP."
  "2026-07-31T15:21:31.897Z","harness:codex","Evidence: the full real-corpus snapshot contains 1,900 closed items; 6 currently retain blocked_by predecessor rows and 185 retain blocks rows, compared with the recorded 1 versus 189 baseline. This change is forward-preserving rather than a provenance-destroying historical backfill. Isolated compiled-CLI acceptance proved blocked_by survives close, reopen, and reclose. Full coverage passed 6,610 tests at 100% statements, branches, functions, and lines."
  "2026-07-31T15:48:29.086Z","harness:codex","Release-gate evidence: build, typecheck, docs/skills, static quality, SDK/package parity, token/retrieval evaluations, 6,610-test coverage at 100/100/100/100, secrets scan, packed npx smoke, and 88-command dogfood all passed from /var/tmp. Sentry/telemetry gate passed with critical=0, high=0 over 14 days and best-effort telemetry. The only pre-push hosted gate result was DeepScan status missing for the uncommitted base SHA; hosted exact-head verification follows on the PR."
learnings[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T14:28:48.217Z","harness:claude-code","A population-level survival split is the cheapest way to separate a deletion defect from an authoring gap, and it needs no code reading. Two spellings of one relation existed in this corpus - blocked_by, which the close path clears, and blocks, which it does not - and their distribution across 1,900 closed items was 1 versus 189. If under-authoring were the cause, both spellings would be scarce. The asymmetry between two encodings of the same fact is the signal; the absolute count of either one is not."
  "2026-07-31T15:21:32.729Z","harness:codex","Lifecycle state and graph provenance are different concerns: terminal transition should clear transient blocked-state scalars while preserving the dependency row that explains historical ordering. Making that distinction an SDK policy prevents presentation routes from silently choosing different semantics."
